    <p class="mh">Work with us</p><a class="mt" href="/careers"><b>Careers</b><small>Clinical education sales for RNs and LPNs</small></a>
    <p class="mh">Account</p><a class="mt" href="/#signup"><b>Join with a school code</b><small>Or request access</small></a><a class="mt" href="/#signin"><b>Sign in</b><small>Already have one</small></a>
   </div></details></nav></div></header><script>(function(){var d=document.querySelector(".mnav");if(!d)return;document.addEventListener("click",function(e){if(d.open&&!d.contains(e.target))d.open=false});document.addEventListener("keydown",function(e){if(e.key==="Escape"&&d.open){d.open=false;d.querySelector("summary").focus()}})})();</script><main><nav class="crumb w" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/nclex-practice-questions">Practice questions</a> › <span>Reduction of Risk Potential</span></nav><section class="hero w"><h1>Reduction of Risk Potential NCLEX Practice Questions</h1><p class="lead">Lowering the risk of complications from tests, procedures, and treatments. Try 8 free questions below, each with the answer, a rationale, and the source it was checked against.</p>
      <p><span class="pill">9–15% of the NCLEX-RN</span><span class="pill">142 questions in Vexcura</span><span class="pill">Reviewed November 2026</span></p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/#practice/RRP">Practice more Reduction of Risk Potential questions</a><a class="btn alt" href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Take the full practice exam</a></div></section>
      <div class="w"><h2>What this category covers</h2><p>Reduction of Risk Potential is one of the eight client needs categories in the NCSBN NCLEX-RN test plan. Expect questions on Lab values, ABGs, Procedure prep, Post-op care, Vital sign changes, and Specimens.</p>
      <div class="tips"><b>How to study Reduction of Risk Potential</b><ul><li>Interpret ABGs with ROME: respiratory opposite, metabolic equal.</li><li>Review positioning and post-procedure care for thoracentesis, paracentesis, liver biopsy, cardiac catheterization, and lumbar puncture.</li><li>Know early warning signs: restlessness and rising heart rate before falling blood pressure; decreasing level of consciousness as the first sign of rising ICP.</li><li>Learn lab values in pairs with the drug or condition that changes them: potassium with diuretics and digoxin, INR with warfarin, glucose with insulin.</li></ul></div>
      <h2>Free Reduction of Risk Potential practice questions</h2><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="rrp4-018">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Multiple choice · Evaluate outcomes</div>
     <p class="stem">Which hemoglobin A1C goal is reasonable for many nonpregnant adults with diabetes?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-018"> <span><b>A.</b> Above 9%</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-018"> <span><b>B.</b> Below 4%</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-018"> <span><b>C.</b> Below 7%</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-018"> <span><b>D.</b> Below 10%</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: C</p><p>An A1C below 7% is a common goal for many nonpregnant adults. Goals are individualized, and less strict targets may be appropriate for older adults or those at risk for hypoglycemia.</p><p class="src">Source: ADA. Standards of Care in Diabetes, Section 6 (glycemic goals)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/hemoglobin-a1c-goal-reasonable-many-nonpregnant-adults-rrp4-018">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="rrp4-019">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">A client is having synchronized cardioversion for atrial fibrillation. What must the nurse confirm before the shock?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-019"> <span><b>A.</b> The client is holding the side rails</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-019"> <span><b>B.</b> The client is not sedated</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-019"> <span><b>C.</b> The defibrillator is set to unsynchronized</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-019"> <span><b>D.</b> The synchronize mode is turned on</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: D</p><p>Synchronization times the shock with the R wave to avoid the vulnerable period that could cause ventricular fibrillation. Clients are sedated and no one touches the bed.</p><p class="src">Source: AHA. Guidelines for CPR and ECC (electrical therapy)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/having-synchronized-cardioversion-atrial-fibrillation-must-confirm-rrp4-019">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="rrp4-009">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Multiple choice · Recognize cues</div>
     <p class="stem">After an upper GI endoscopy, which finding should the nurse report right away?</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-009"> <span><b>A.</b> Sharp chest or abdominal pain with fever</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-009"> <span><b>B.</b> Mild sore throat</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-009"> <span><b>C.</b> Feeling drowsy for an hour</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-009"> <span><b>D.</b> Bloating that improves after passing gas</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: A</p><p>Severe pain, fever, or difficulty swallowing after endoscopy may signal perforation. A mild sore throat and bloating are expected.</p><p class="src">Source: Lewis's Medical-Surgical Nursing, 13th ed. (endoscopy)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/upper-gi-endoscopy-finding-report-right-away-rrp4-009">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="sata" id="rrp4-013">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Select all that apply · Recognize cues</div>
     <p class="stem">Which findings should the nurse include in a neurovascular check of a casted leg? Select all that apply.</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-rrp4-013"> <span><b>A.</b> Capillary refill</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-rrp4-013"> <span><b>B.</b> Ability to move the toes</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-rrp4-013"> <span><b>C.</b> Blood glucose</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-rrp4-013"> <span><b>D.</b> Bowel sounds</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-rrp4-013"> <span><b>E.</b> Sensation in the toes</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-rrp4-013"> <span><b>F.</b> Color and temperature of the toes</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answers: A, B, E, F</p><p>Neurovascular checks include color, temperature, capillary refill, pulses, sensation, movement, and pain, to detect compartment syndrome or impaired circulation early.</p><p class="src">Source: Lewis's Medical-Surgical Nursing, 13th ed. (fracture care)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/findings-include-neurovascular-check-casted-leg-select-rrp4-013">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="rrp4-012">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Multiple choice · Recognize cues</div>
     <p class="stem">During neuro checks on a client with a head injury, the nurse finds a newly fixed and dilated right pupil. What does this suggest?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-012"> <span><b>A.</b> Effects of eye drops only</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-012"> <span><b>B.</b> A normal finding after head injury</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-012"> <span><b>C.</b> Improvement in the client's condition</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-012"> <span><b>D.</b> Increasing intracranial pressure with possible brain herniation</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: D</p><p>A new unilateral fixed, dilated pupil suggests compression of cranial nerve III from herniation. This is an emergency requiring immediate notification.</p><p class="src">Source: Lewis's Medical-Surgical Nursing, 13th ed. (head injury)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/neuro-checks-head-injury-finds-newly-fixed-rrp4-012">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="rrp4-011">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">Where should the nurse listen to obtain an apical pulse in an adult?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-011"> <span><b>A.</b> Second intercostal space at the right sternal border</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-011"> <span><b>B.</b> Over the radial artery</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-011"> <span><b>C.</b> Fifth intercostal space at the left midclavicular line</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-011"> <span><b>D.</b> At the xiphoid process</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: C</p><p>The apical pulse is heard at the point of maximal impulse, at the left 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line.</p><p class="src">Source: Potter &amp; Perry. Fundamentals of Nursing, 12th ed. (vital signs)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/where-listen-obtain-apical-pulse-adult-rrp4-011">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="calc" data-a="80" data-tol="0" id="rrp3-010">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Calculation · Analyze cues</div>
     <p class="stem">A client's blood pressure is 120/60 mm Hg. What is the mean arterial pressure (MAP)?</p><div class="calc"><label for="q-rrp3-010">Answer</label><input id="q-rrp3-010" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off"> <span>mm Hg</span></div>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Answer: 80 mm Hg</p><p>MAP ≈ (SBP + 2 × DBP) ÷ 3 = (120 + 120) ÷ 3 = 80 mm Hg. A MAP of at least 65 mm Hg is generally needed to perfuse organs.</p><p class="src">Source: Lewis's Medical-Surgical Nursing, 13th ed. (hemodynamic monitoring)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/s-blood-pressure-120-60-mm-hg-rrp3-010">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="rrp4-005">
     <div class="meta">Reduction of Risk Potential · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">How should a client collect a sputum specimen for culture?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-005"> <span><b>A.</b> At bedtime after using mouthwash</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-005"> <span><b>B.</b> By spitting saliva into the cup</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-005"> <span><b>C.</b> After brushing teeth with toothpaste and eating breakfast</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-rrp4-005"> <span><b>D.</b> Early in the morning, after rinsing the mouth with water, by coughing deeply</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: D</p><p>Early morning specimens contain more secretions. Rinsing with water (not mouthwash) reduces contamination, and deep coughing produces sputum rather than saliva.</p><p class="src">Source: Pagana. Mosby's Manual of Diagnostic and Laboratory Tests (sputum culture)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/collect-sputum-specimen-culture-rrp4-005">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article>
      <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/#practice/RRP">Practice more Reduction of Risk Potential questions</a><a class="btn alt" href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Take the full practice exam</a></div><div class="split rev"><div class="st"><h2>See your Reduction of Risk Potential score after a full exam</h2><p>The full adaptive practice exam runs 85 to 150 items on a five-hour clock and stops by the same rules as the NCLEX. Results break down by all 8 client needs categories, with a pass-likelihood estimate, so students know exactly what to study next. It's free for students through a licensed nursing school, and every plan includes everything.</p><p><a href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">The full practice exam</a></p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/exam-results-cats-1000.webp" srcset="/img/exam-results-cats-1600.webp 1600w, /img/exam-results-cats-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="Full practice exam results by NCLEX client needs category, from below to above the passing standard" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>Full-exam results by client needs category. Demo student.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--muted)">Took an outside NCLEX-readiness test at school? <a href="/ati-score-report-study-plan">Turn your score report into a study plan</a> built from the topics it lists.</p><h2>Other NCLEX categories</h2><div class="grid"><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/management-of-care"><b>Management of Care</b><small>15–21% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/safety-and-infection-control"><b>Safety and Infection Prevention and Control</b><small>10–16% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/health-promotion-and-maintenance"><b>Health Promotion and Maintenance</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/psychosocial-integrity"><b>Psychosocial Integrity</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/basic-care-and-comfort"><b>Basic Care and Comfort</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/pharmacology"><b>Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies</b><small>13–19% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/physiological-adaptation"><b>Physiological Adaptation</b><small>11–17% of the exam</small></a></div><h2>Related</h2><ul class="rel"><li><a href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Full adaptive NCLEX-RN practice exam</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-prioritization-questions">NCLEX prioritization questions</a></li><li><a href="/next-generation-nclex">Next Generation NCLEX (NGN) guide</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-study-guides">Free NCLEX study guides (PDF)</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-pass-guarantee">NCLEX pass guarantee: registration fee back if you qualify and don't pass</a></li></ul></div><section class="band vxs-band"><div class="vxs-bg" data-vxs-i="0" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="vxs-sh" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="w"><h2>Bring Vexcura to your nursing program</h2><p>1,220 cited NCLEX-RN questions, adaptive practice exams, 8 NGN case studies, an Epic-style academic EHR and a clinical instructor workspace. The school pays; students and instructors are always free, and every plan includes everything. Students: ask your instructor, or join with your school's code.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/for-nursing-schools">See Vexcura for nursing schools</a><a class="btn alt" href="/for-nursing-schools#pricing">Pricing</a><a class="btn alt" href="/#signup">Join with a school code</a></div></div></section></main><footer class="foot"><div class="w"><div class="cols">
